# VANTA admin credentials
#
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and set real values. `.env.local` is gitignored
# and must never be committed.
#
#   cp .env.local.example .env.local
#
# Restart the dev server after changing any of these.

# Username for signing in at /admin/login
ADMIN_USERNAME=admin

# Password for signing in at /admin/login.
# Use a long, random value — this is the only credential protecting the admin.
ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-to-a-long-random-password

# Secret used to sign the admin session cookie (HS256 JWT).
# MUST be at least 32 characters. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
# Changing this immediately invalidates all existing sessions.
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=change-me-to-a-random-string-of-at-least-32-characters

# The site's public address, used to build share-preview links for WhatsApp,
# Instagram, Slack and search engines. No trailing slash.
#
# Leave unset in development — it falls back to http://localhost:3000.
# On Vercel it is detected automatically and this can stay unset there too.
# Anywhere else, set it to the real domain before launch or link previews
# will point at the wrong host and show no image.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://vanta.in

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Postgres powers customer accounts, server-saved bags and wishlists, and the
# Postgres content store. It is a capability, not a requirement: leave all of
# this unset and the storefront runs exactly as before — the catalogue comes
# from the JSON file, the bag stays in the browser, and the account pages say
# accounts aren't available yet.
#
# DATABASE_URL is the POOLED connection string. On a serverless host every
# request can be its own process, and a direct connection per request exhausts
# the server's limit long before traffic does.
#
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@host/db?sslmode=require
#
# DIRECT_DATABASE_URL is the UNPOOLED one, used only by `prisma migrate`. A
# transaction-mode pooler cannot hold the advisory lock a migration takes, so
# migrating through the pooled URL hangs or fails without saying why. Only
# needed where the two differ (Neon, Supabase, PgBouncer).
#
#   DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@host/db?sslmode=require
#
# Which content-store adapter runs. Defaults to "postgres" when DATABASE_URL is
# set and "file" otherwise. Override to keep content on disk while still using
# the database for accounts.
#
# Switching drivers moves nothing — the two stores are separate places. Run
# `npm run content:import` once to copy an existing .content/site.json into
# Postgres, or the site silently falls back to the /data seed and looks as
# though every edit was lost.
#
#   CONTENT_STORE_DRIVER=postgres

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare Turnstile (captcha)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Keys come from the Turnstile section of the Cloudflare dashboard.
#
# The site key is public — it is rendered into the page and there is nothing to
# protect. The SECRET KEY MUST NEVER BE PREFIXED NEXT_PUBLIC_: anything with
# that prefix is inlined into the browser bundle, and a leaked secret key lets
# anyone verify their own tokens against your account.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=0x4AAAAAAA...
#   TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=0x4AAAAAAA...
#
# Behaviour when TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY is unset differs deliberately by surface:
#
#   - Customer sign-in and registration SKIP the check, so development works
#     without a Cloudflare account.
#   - /admin/login REFUSES TO SIGN ANYONE IN and says the server is
#     misconfigured. An admin login that silently drops its captcha the moment
#     an env var goes missing is worse than one that never had it, because
#     nothing looks wrong and nobody finds out.
#
# Cloudflare publishes dummy keys for local testing. The always-passing pair:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=1x00000000000000000000AA
#   TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Razorpay (online payments)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Keys come from Razorpay Dashboard → Account & Settings → API Keys. Test-mode
# keys start `rzp_test_`, live ones `rzp_live_`. Nothing here is optional as a
# group: with none of it set, the storefront still works and checkout simply
# offers cash on delivery, saying so plainly.
#
# NONE of these may ever be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_. The key id is technically
# public — Razorpay's own checkout script receives it in the browser — but it
# is handed over by a server action rather than inlined, so that there is
# exactly one place keys are read from and no chance of the secret following
# the same pattern by accident.
#
#   RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=rzp_test_xxxxxxxxxxxxxx
#   RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
#
# The webhook secret is separate from the API keys and is the string you type
# into Dashboard → Settings → Webhooks when creating the endpoint. It is what
# signs the payload; without it every webhook is rejected, which means no order
# is ever marked paid.
#
#   RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=a-long-random-string-you-choose
#
# Webhook endpoint to register:      https://your-domain/api/webhooks/razorpay
# Events to subscribe to:            payment.captured, payment.failed, order.paid
#
# Locally, Razorpay cannot reach http://localhost. Tunnel it — `npx untun` or
# ngrok — and register the tunnel's URL. Without a webhook reaching the app,
# test payments succeed in Razorpay and the order stays "awaiting payment"
# forever, which is the system working as designed, not a bug.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secret encryption at rest
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Encrypts credentials that have to be stored in a *recoverable* form, which
# today means exactly one thing: the cached Shiprocket bearer token.
#
# Everything else this app stores is hashed — admin passwords through scrypt,
# customer session tokens and Turnstile tokens as SHA-256 digests — because
# those are only ever compared. A Shiprocket token has to be sent back to
# Shiprocket verbatim, so a digest is useless and the value itself must
# survive. AES-256-GCM under this key is the alternative to a plaintext column.
#
# Exactly 32 bytes, base64 or hex. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
#   SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY=BASE64_OR_HEX_OF_32_RANDOM_BYTES
#
# Unset is safe, not broken. With no key the token cache is simply skipped —
# the app keeps a token in process memory and logs in again on each cold start.
# It never falls back to writing the plaintext, because a silent downgrade of
# exactly the property this exists to provide is worse than no feature at all.
#
# Rotating this key costs one extra Shiprocket login and nothing else. Every
# value it protects is a cache of something re-derivable, so an unreadable row
# is discarded rather than mourned — there is no re-encryption pass to run and
# no data to lose. Do NOT put anything irreplaceable behind it without first
# giving it a real key-rotation story.
#
# What it does and doesn't buy: it protects against a leaked database dump, a
# too-broad read grant, a dump pulled through an injection. It does not protect
# against an attacker who already has this environment — they would have
# SHIPROCKET_PASSWORD too and could just log in. The two leak through different
# channels; closing the more common one is the point.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shiprocket (courier delivery)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Credentials are for an API USER, not your main Shiprocket login. Create one
# at Settings -> API -> Add New API User; the email must differ from your
# account email, and the password is shown once.
#
# All three are needed together. With any of them missing, the storefront still
# takes orders exactly as before — the pincode check says it can't check, and
# orders queue in /admin/orders until the courier is connected. Nothing about
# buying depends on Shiprocket being reachable, by design. See DECISIONS §27.
#
#   SHIPROCKET_EMAIL=api-user@yourdomain.com
#   SHIPROCKET_PASSWORD=the-password-shown-once
#
# The name of a pickup location that ALREADY EXISTS in your Shiprocket account
# (Settings -> Company -> Pickup Addresses). They reject a name they don't know.
#
#   SHIPROCKET_PICKUP_LOCATION=Primary
#
# The pincode that location ships from. Rates and delivery estimates are
# calculated between this and the customer's pincode, so a wrong value quotes
# the wrong delivery date for every product page.
#
#   SHIPROCKET_PICKUP_PINCODE=110030
#
# Parcel dimensions and weight. Shiprocket requires all four and rejects zeroes,
# and this catalogue records none of them — so one conservative parcel is used
# for everything. Their rate is charged on volumetric weight, so under-declaring
# means an invoice that doesn't match the quote.
#
#   SHIPROCKET_PARCEL_LENGTH_CM=30
#   SHIPROCKET_PARCEL_BREADTH_CM=25
#   SHIPROCKET_PARCEL_HEIGHT_CM=8
#   SHIPROCKET_PARCEL_WEIGHT_KG=0.5
#
# The token sent with their tracking webhook, as an `x-api-key` header. Choose
# any long random string and paste the same one into Settings -> API -> Webhooks.
#
# Their side treats it as optional. This app does not: with it unset the webhook
# route refuses every request, because a public endpoint that rewrites order
# state without authentication is not an acceptable default.
#
#   SHIPROCKET_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=a-long-random-string-you-choose
#
# Webhook URL to register:  https://your-domain/api/webhooks/courier
#
# Note the path says "courier", not "shiprocket". Their documentation forbids
# the keywords "shiprocket", "kartrocket", "sr" and "kr" anywhere in the URL,
# and a URL containing one is rejected at registration.
#
# Where a customer follows their parcel. Optional — defaults to Shiprocket's
# public tracking page. Set this to your own branded tracking domain if you have
# one; `{awb}` is substituted.
#
#   SHIPROCKET_TRACKING_URL=https://track.yourdomain.com/{awb}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduled jobs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Protects /api/courier/sync, which drains the courier push queue and refreshes
# tracking for shipments still in flight. Sent either as `x-cron-secret` or as
# `Authorization: Bearer <secret>` — Vercel Cron uses the latter.
#
# Unset means the route refuses everything. It spends money on someone else's
# API, so an open version of it is a way for a stranger to run up a bill.
#
#   CRON_SECRET=a-long-random-string-you-choose
#
# On Vercel, add to vercel.json:
#
#   { "crons": [{ "path": "/api/courier/sync", "schedule": "*/15 * * * *" }] }
